{"type":"message","id":"a68bd2d5","parentId":"8f989d16","timestamp":"2026-03-18T13:27:46.125Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Structuring the answer**\n\nI need to provide a structured response that starts with red flags, looking at the worsening months, prior RCC/post-nephrectomy, hematuria, and fatigue. I want to avoid overclaiming while still expressing concern. Also, I should mention that I've reviewed records from a patient without needing to dive into citations. Maybe, it’s beneficial to conclude with just one specific question at the end for clarity. This way, I can keep the communication focused!","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0955faedd60c0a550169baa844f9148191a0340a7cf770034f\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"encrypted_content\":\"gAAAAABpuqhI7FFCf-wxwJqo_3o-zZsUCyAVNdujnOL4Lpa6DIslC3EVGsNvlaxI3kNKoKbg_DQPrcl9HMg78rhATSLkrFgjwbxWx81NbybQZsLJa_90JvlzzvmxvRxAhXdBxlaVZE_48YmT8WkZaNKtJBhElgsS-IJlF3m73XATEfUd9SlZInFdRczEVP9wV4DJkJi9a--TsJEzvtVkmezZ6nyez7mr6trJ-YV3752XHFqBad-wgX5XFNCkJXzvVqIwOwqMxu8ujkKVA-kOtcQ7dYOEKya3h5ruTXOvrCf92qx_xLwwtVVJjyVA6-tL91x-7d0Mz54d_gXKfb19kh8onPHpybBED8DEnoAecZX1aCvClVNmOtGGwpxcdEXk6zO-Jmf-mTQwhr6MIkq7KWFNkVJZD-WQp-YFScAG2HFubh76T6czAQopQQDZnZQvw-_vSy-oixhpSiPDWOoJMvCglR_kLUnITzrVDqu-3-Rtz86-fkQFuYtdc8mGKD4Ynh_Up-7N7Q5uLgbyFnsFj9slzgk1srI_ZBe3F9Fz-Y0mz3IDV1Pt4iqDU4y5K7utShF1pDUjDE5zOcX2ZciD3Ufw-pagb8qYjuWylN7B8Wfj-DX6cX0phT11hr87qBtcEjhdQ_BTtge5caHqsGhef0Ni0TEJ-fFRQH63HJXKA27yO_VcxOssPbAoPjMi4-OvchgebqDjACIb_AfzTb21fxwZ_fkgFYaA8_TWztUmF1KfTNBZ-6EFAXqrx8JroM2ZA1csgYMgacr0jB6YYvsac2Tw_w3VBj3Pt8Yv46QYSeSDH3yaex2X4gC2YRxNWaWDrjsBO1nQq1UUNco4w2Dg1EYohirQGBz0ItxcZPexlCmMmmZr7u1su_Gs3xMn_nOJ_uktKKw3GrKQ6tKP5GaClKEeggkHqHH9AXJ520OKWhpnXYZdx3AjlTipkPqeENIRTAquRXQUkcdL6CD2w1zYehsYMQHHROxL1oOMI_vGvHnGN-OgnVg2mtKVCQyeuIo-nvNdcycoCRQ5YkAmga-UZNHJTOCEIj2ZLnppAff8x8P2zNi3WyVu4j1yYN2eeHaTtzFg_3ywTh59jOdaS-z6ac1GOLUtFQufM0fQrcsRLRQHbs_ZMDoIvvRaPqd0rvsfp8bvFUnsGBhy_7bdCKHJsYaPs-p-rUBXJA==\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Structuring the answer**\\n\\nI need to provide a structured response that starts with red flags, looking at the worsening months, prior RCC/post-nephrectomy, hematuria, and fatigue. I want to avoid overclaiming while still expressing concern. Also, I should mention that I've reviewed records from a patient without needing to dive into citations. Maybe, it’s beneficial to conclude with just one specific question at the end for clarity. This way, I can keep the communication focused!\"}]}"},{"type":"text","text":"[[reply_to_current]] ### Clinical picture\nYou’re describing **progressive exhaustion** plus a **persistent dull right lower back/flank ache** that has been getting worse over months. From the record you previously shared, I can see a history of **right-sided renal cell carcinoma**, **post-nephrectomy follow-up**, **CKD stage 3 / reduced kidney function trend**, **recent mild hematuria**, and a genomic/family background that increases kidney-related risk.\n\n### Most likely possibilities\n1. **Kidney/urinary-tract recurrence or other right-sided renal complication**\n   - This is a key concern because your current pain is **right-sided**, persistent, and worsening, and your records already show prior **right kidney cancer**, prior hematuria, and reduced kidney function.\n\n2. **Post-surgical or chronic flank/back pain related to the kidney area**\n   - A dull ache over months can fit chronic pain after kidney surgery or scarring, but this would not fully explain worsening fatigue by itself.\n\n3. **Progression of kidney dysfunction / CKD-related symptoms**\n   - Fatigue can fit reduced kidney function or anemia, and your past records show creatinine/eGFR changes consistent with ongoing renal follow-up.\n\n4. **Musculoskeletal low back pain**\n   - This is still possible, especially if the pain changes with movement or posture, but given your history it is **not the first explanation to assume** without excluding kidney-related causes.\n\n### Confidence / uncertainty note\nI’m **more concerned than reassured** here because the symptom pattern overlaps with your known kidney history. I cannot tell from symptoms alone whether this is recurrence, CKD-related change, post-surgical pain, or a non-kidney back problem. The most useful next clarifiers would be **whether you still have blood in the urine, whether the pain feels deep in the flank, and whether there are systemic symptoms like weight loss or fever**.\n\n### Red flags / urgent concerns\nPlease seek **urgent same-day medical review** sooner rather than later if any of these are happening:\n- **visible blood in the urine**\n- **fever**, chills, or feeling acutely unwell\n- **rapidly worsening pain**\n- **nausea/vomiting**\n- **reduced urine output**\n- new swelling, shortness of breath, marked weakness, or dizziness\n\nBecause of your kidney cancer history and kidney function history, even without emergency symptoms, this does sound like something that should be **promptly reviewed by your treating clinician/urology/oncology team** rather than watched casually.\n\n### Recommended next questions\nTo narrow this properly, I’ll ask the 5 highest-yield questions one by one.\n\n**Question 1/5:**  \nHave you noticed **any blood in your urine** recently — either visible red/pink urine, darker tea-colored urine, or has anyone told you you still have microscopic hematuria?","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0955faedd60c0a550169baa84871948191af4d860d70bf3f66\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":21207,"output":660,"cacheRead":11392,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":33259,"cost":{"input":0.0530175,"output":0.0099,"cacheRead":0.002848,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0657655}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1773840450460}}
{"type":"message","id":"e4bf64ae","parentId":"a68bd2d5","timestamp":"2026-03-18T13:29:42.969Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[media attached: /home/node/.openclaw/media/inbound/file_8---9ec3dd69-75ec-4ce0-8435-a7ca81facc9a.jpg (image/jpeg) | /home/node/.openclaw/media/inbound/file_8---9ec3dd69-75ec-4ce0-8435-a7ca81facc9a.jpg]\nTo send an image back, prefer the message tool (media/path/filePath). If you must inline, use MEDIA:https://example.com/image.jpg (spaces ok, quote if needed) or a safe relative path like MEDIA:./image.jpg. Avoid absolute paths (MEDIA:/...) and ~ paths — they are blocked for security. Keep caption in the text body.\nConversation info (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"message_id\": \"376\",\n  \"sender_id\": \"495506013\",\n  \"sender\": \"Matteo Cicalese\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"Wed 2026-03-18 13:29 UTC\"\n}\n```\n\nSender (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"Matteo Cicalese (495506013)\",\n  \"id\": \"495506013\",\n  \"name\": \"Matteo Cicalese\",\n  \"username\": \"CicaMatt\"\n}\n```\n\nlets bypass the questions, can you analyze this new CT scan i had?"},{"type":"image","data":"","mimeType":"image/jpeg"}],"timestamp":1773840582965}}
{"type":"message","id":"164597a6","parentId":"e4bf64ae","timestamp":"2026-03-18T13:29:48.108Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Evaluating skill for CT scan analysis**\n\nI need to assess whether the kidney-pipeline-execution skill is required since the user wants to analyze a CT scan. It seems like this specific skill is indeed necessary because it integrates the kidney pipeline on the image. I want to ensure I'm using the right skill, so I'll check the details of the skill before proceeding. It's important to be precise in my approach!","thinkingSignature":"{\"id\":\"rs_0955faedd60c0a550169baa8c8a7608191b2b06126c038a3ca\",\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"encrypted_content\":\"gAAAAABpuqjL6Tl5SP2a9OkfvWRf_3Y3rbBKTRMsvuilmY8CU-qxSAExK8MBm2QM2HRRROB-VdoGQ7Oa5bnViq5nnEnm5-GU2Sq-fH5e0V3O5laqfuqbPLWPwxpm5SGDka_1udRl0MRxvEFtIVzBjC0APHND2njqztc7GfanaeBA7PSfbyzRM2YGQFFEMqHoPGzzvD3u2p_G8L4MZTjXwL6-uvPYR79RrzdXMI6ps97CGoefO_LL1yA-F8eCowJx_EAZSuGDVIxA1M9DbWG2Y11pLkHBk6BwZRWMe9hqwTgk7xAnMWEoXVgUF6p7RsI_yCL8oQLng8PEdnm8muJDwRrRIz7DUuLLrGR4yxEJimfZUapc5ybUHUJ6X696KC2RdfY5iF0Blxmnh2EaLG9R91X4vVpR2cEZuzc2zL7s0Hx8LH_97NgRDaORdaVyWx1qEiIiKx9yfggDuBxqRMOBO39eRHEgwd7e3Sh-Wrb1vwmPMuXXjnn8BgRk34vMnAHG6FLKSLbjk6Xk3nQnJvGunGMsFD4mT5rdF1YPikpzlAYVV0IigbjqE8q-i7BofVxcmdPmQX2b7fKEtfcb_Vr5nJJF0iWlBgt5RQzn-QMvkIyJgzaypCIQPwmB-1c7JwFO2yIDJxWAeQ8Oc368zyVWjPSReCjO5pL9qQdrPaRUk4_vk3XnQbRHUnmA148obs45IzKNM0KDqRzE7z8PXUYL6MVNcyjQpqZbpsYPm292KJckLOKxmt71bl4KT2E5UxEFV1XJQSuDgfRaefk5FSUqDlkJmiVGmFfDi6qDLQrDqWFs_S1IXVoLZP53jm-hP67Km7-jfCEnocAjKl6pjFf9ynNo48M4b1ST-veNjRqjOWejNZp0lgOvBOV9khk57OTPhUPOMS3pBiKRNqEf_XlEnD85YoFsphajvNM1CIsaFLIT4I3yG0O8EwRIkYG9fmE1bG2U5ubG8LqeKLHDP2HE8_wOGGvQ44sJYiYgEegUBhZcJym719qGWVAZmKL-1HED9LFL0LaOH-3r6bngxdO0Z5_cnxVTCTOHhbNOM139jwFPUxKNvoqn1-g=\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Evaluating skill for CT scan analysis**\\n\\nI need to assess whether the kidney-pipeline-execution skill is required since the user wants to analyze a CT scan. It seems like this specific skill is indeed necessary because it integrates the kidney pipeline on the image. I want to ensure I'm using the right skill, so I'll check the details of the skill before proceeding. It's important to be precise in my approach!\"}]}"},{"type":"text","text":"I can take a preliminary look, but a single CT image is not enough for a safe radiology-style interpretation on its own. I’ll use the kidney imaging pipeline to extract structured signals from the scan and then I’ll translate the result clearly for you.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0955faedd60c0a550169baa8cb60f481918395adedd91814dd\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_tYd4J4UPvKz8liONw8fmqpch|fc_0955faedd60c0a550169baa8cb97b08191816298182eb3aec1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"~/.openclaw/skills/kidney-pipeline-execution/SKILL.md"},"partialJson":"{\"path\":\"~/.openclaw/skills/kidney-pipeline-execution/SKILL.md\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":33661,"output":133,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":33794,"cost":{"input":0.0841525,"output":0.0019950000000000002,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0861475}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1773840582966}}
{"type":"message","id":"d9b4664c","parentId":"164597a6","timestamp":"2026-03-18T13:29:48.124Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_tYd4J4UPvKz8liONw8fmqpch|fc_0955faedd60c0a550169baa8cb97b08191816298182eb3aec1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: kidney-pipeline-execution\ndescription: Execute the kidney cancer detection pipeline by first uploading a user-provided image with the same upload flow and Telegram user id logic used by the kidney-cancer-detection skill, then replacing the image-path placeholder in the stored prototype request JSON and sending it to the prototype execution endpoint. Use when a user wants to run this integrated kidney pipeline on an image and inspect either the raw response or the expected key outputs from the kidney cancer detector, the Image Analyzer, and the X-AI component.\n---\n\n# Kidney Pipeline Execution\n\nUpload the local image first, then execute the fixed kidney cancer detection prototype payload with the uploaded remote image path substituted into the request JSON. This pipeline includes a Kidney Cancer Detection model, an Image Analyzer component, and an integrated X-AI component downstream.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Determine the local image to analyze.\n   - Prefer an explicit local file path when one is available.\n   - If no path is provided, use the most recent supported image under `/home/node/.openclaw/media/inbound`.\n2. Require the Telegram user id.\n   - Pass it as `--telegram-user-id` or set `TELEGRAM_USER_ID`.\n   - Use that value as the upload request's `user_id` query parameter.\n   - Also use that same numeric Telegram id as the default Telegram send target so the downloaded image goes back to the same DM conversation unless `--telegram-target` explicitly overrides it.\n3. Upload the image with a `POST multipart/form-data` request to:\n   - `http://looporchestra.sytes.net:4001/nodes/input/upload?storage_ref=nodes_bucket&local_file_path=upload%2F&user_id=<telegram-user-id>`\n4. Read the upload response JSON and build the remote image path by concatenating:\n   - `path + filename`\n5. Replace the `<image_path_here>` placeholder in the fixed prototype JSON with the uploaded remote image path.\n6. Send the full resulting JSON payload to:\n   - `http://looporchestra.sytes.net:4001/admin/prototype_execution/prototype_execution/`\n7. Read the analyzer image URL only from `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.file_path`.\n   - Treat `file_path` as a sibling of `cam_explanation` in the live response format.\n   - Extract the URL from that field.\n   - Download the image it points to and save the canonical copy under `/home/node/openclaw-shared/kidney_heatmaps`.\n   - Also write a reply-ready copy into the active workspace so the agent can attach it with the native chat reply path (for example `MEDIA:./kidney-pipeline-last-heatmap.jpeg`).\n   - Prefer that native inline reply attachment path over a separate provider-side send.\n8. Return the raw response inside a minimal envelope that includes:\n   - `uploaded_image_path`\n   - `endpoint`\n   - `http_status`\n   - `content_type`\n   - `response`\n   - when available, also include `analyzer_image_url`, `analyzer_image_local_path`, `analyzer_image_content_type`, `reply_media_path`, and `reply_media_name` after downloading the image pointed to by the analyzer `file_path` field\n9. When summarizing the result for the user, expect and prioritize these wanted output fields from the raw response:\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.classes`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.confidence`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.prediction`\n   - every available `cam_...` field under the Image Analyzer tracking parameters, including the full `cam_metrics` object and any other `cam_*` entries such as `cam_explanation`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.confidence_interpretation`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.recommended_next_steps`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.references`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.summary`\n   - `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.visual_evidence`\n10. Present those wanted outputs in a clean human-readable summary instead of low-level JSON-path labels. Prefer labels such as `Kidney cancer class`, `Confidence`, `Image Analyzer prediction`, `CAM coverage`, `CAM center ratio`, `CAM left-right asymmetry`, `CAM top-bottom asymmetry`, `CAM explanation`, `Confidence interpretation`, `Recommended next steps`, `References`, `Summary`, and `Visual evidence`.\n11. Do not include low-level run details by default in user-facing replies. Omit technical execution metadata such as upload paths, endpoint names, HTTP status, node ids, workflow ids, raw file paths, and similar run-internal fields unless the user explicitly asks for technical details or the raw response.\n12. Mention, when present, that the broader raw response may also include an analyzer output `file_path` plus X-AI explanatory fields such as `confidence_interpretation`, `recommended_next_steps`, `references`, `summary`, and `visual_evidence`.\n\n## Fixed request values\n\nKeep these values static:\n- `storage_ref`: `nodes_bucket`\n- `local_file_path`: `upload/`\n- execution endpoint: `http://looporchestra.sytes.net:4001/admin/prototype_execution/prototype_execution/`\n- prototype payload: use the fixed JSON stored in `references/request-template.json`\n\n## What this skill runs\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/pipeline_execution_tool.py --telegram-user-id <telegram-user-id> [--reply-media-dir <workspace-dir>] [local-image-path]\n```\n\nIf `local-image-path` is omitted, the script automatically selects the newest supported image from `/home/node/.openclaw/media/inbound`.\n\n## Upload response handling\n\nGiven an upload response like:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"path\": \"upload/2026-03-15_14.52.42_5EdSbYnzCrKdhainPvGx/\",\n  \"filename\": \"9dd106ff-65ad-4a16-a381-6d76e4845c9c.jpg\",\n  \"column_names\": null\n}\n```\n\nBuild:\n\n```text\nimage_path = \"upload/2026-03-15_14.52.42_5EdSbYnzCrKdhainPvGx/9dd106ff-65ad-4a16-a381-6d76e4845c9c.jpg\"\n```\n\nUse that full value anywhere the request JSON contains `<image_path_here>`.\n\n## Expected output\n\nExpect a stable high-level wrapper plus a kidney-pipeline-oriented response body.\n\nReturn the endpoint response inside this wrapper:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"uploaded_image_path\": \"string\",\n  \"endpoint\": \"string\",\n  \"http_status\": 200,\n  \"content_type\": \"application/json\",\n  \"response\": {}\n}\n```\n\nFor user-facing summaries, treat these as the primary expected outputs when present:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"wanted_output_fields\": {\n    \"kidney_cancer_class\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.classes\",\n    \"kidney_cancer_confidence\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.confidence\",\n    \"image_analyzer_prediction\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.prediction\",\n    \"cam_metrics\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.cam_metrics\",\n    \"cam_explanation\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.cam_explanation\",\n    \"confidence_interpretation\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.confidence_interpretation\",\n    \"recommended_next_steps\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.recommended_next_steps\",\n    \"references\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.references\",\n    \"summary\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.summary\",\n    \"visual_evidence\": \"response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.visual_evidence\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen `cam_metrics` is present, include all of its subfields in the wanted output summary, such as:\n- `coverage`\n- `center_ratio`\n- `lr_asym`\n- `tb_asym`\n\nAlso include these secondary fields when useful:\n- `response.workflows[0].branches[0].nodes[0].children[0].children[0].tracking.parameters.file_path`\n- the downloaded analyzer image metadata exposed by the script as `analyzer_image_url`, `analyzer_image_local_path`, `analyzer_image_content_type`, `reply_media_path`, and `reply_media_name`\n\nWhen `reply_media_path` is present, attach the image in the same user-facing reply using the native chat/media path instead of relying on a separate outbound provider send.\n\nWhen `recommended_next_steps` is present, preserve the list order. When `references` is present, preserve each item's available fields such as `title`, `author`, `year`, and `url`.\n\nDo not present the summary as raw JSON-path labels unless the user explicitly asks for raw field paths. Default to a cleaner format with readable labels and grouped findings.\nDo not include low-level run details in the default summary. Reserve upload paths, endpoint values, HTTP status, execution metadata, node identifiers, and other run-internal fields for explicit technical or debugging requests.\n\nIf the endpoint returns non-JSON text, return that text as the `response` value.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1773840588116}}
